// A Map that lives outside a region, mutated inside it, read after it.
//
// This lives in test/rv/ because the RV32I column is the one it was written for,
// and parity has no RV column. It used to live here for a second reason that has
// expired: the Wasm backend REFUSED the shape outright ("map_set inside a region
// block ...", memory-model.md 3.5) -- two honest answers, and a parity row can
// only hold one. Since v0.1.458 Wasm accepts it and agrees (measured: `done /
// value / 50 / 1` on the interpreter, C and Wasm alike), because a store into a
// container older than the block now stops the block's release short of it
// rather than being rejected.
//
// The map's cell predates the region; `map_set` allocates a new cons node for
// the entry. On backends where map internals live in the map's own arena the
// region is irrelevant. On RV32I the Map is prelude code and its node comes
// from the bump heap -- so a region that rolls the bump pointer back declares
// the node reusable while the map still points at it, and the allocations
// after the region overwrite it. mere-ruby runs every top-level statement
// inside `region STMT`, which made one `map_set` per statement enough to
// corrupt its constant table.
//
// The allocations after the region are what make the test able to fail: a
// dangling node whose memory nobody reuses still holds its bytes, and reading
// it back looks like a pass.
let m = map_new ();
let tag = region R {
  let _ = map_set m "k" ("val" ++ "ue") in
  "done"
};
// reuse pressure: enough fresh blocks to land on any rolled-back node
let rec churn = fn (i: int) -> fn (acc: str list) ->
  if i <= 0 then acc else churn (i - 1) (Cons ("block" ++ str_of_int i, acc));
let junk = churn 50 Nil;
let rec count = fn (l: str list) -> match l with Nil -> 0 | Cons (_, r) -> 1 + count r;
let _ = print tag;
let _ = print (map_get m "k");
let _ = print_int (count junk);
let _ = print_int (map_len m);
